      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve for \\( n \\) in the equation \\( |n + 6| = 2 - n \\), we need to consider the definition of the absolute value function. The absolute value equation \\( |x| = y \\) means that \\( x = y \\) or \\( x = -y \\). Applying this to our problem, we have two cases to consider:\n\n### Case 1: \\( n + 6 = 2 - n \\)\nWe will solve this by adding \\( n \\) to both sides of the equation:\n\\[ n + 6 + n = 2 - n + n \\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[ 2n + 6 = 2 \\]\nNext, we subtract 6 from both sides:\n\\[ 2n + 6 - 6 = 2 - 6 \\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[ 2n = -4 \\]\nFinally, we divide both sides by 2:\n\\[ n = \\frac{-4}{2} \\]\nThis gives:\n\\[ n = -2 \\]\n\n### Case 2: \\( n + 6 = -(2 - n) \\)\nWe will solve this by first simplifying the right-hand side:\n\\[ n + 6 = -2 + n \\]\nNext, we subtract \\( n \\) from both sides:\n\\[ n + 6 - n = -2 + n - n \\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[ 6 = -2 \\]\nThis is a contradiction, indicating that there is no solution to this case.\n\n### Conclusion\nThe only solution to the equation \\( |n + 6| = 2 - n \\) is from Case 1, where \\( n = -2 \\).\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-2}\n\\]",
