      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "- The left-hand side of the equation is the absolute value expression which means that the expression inside the absolute value must either be $2-n$, if it’s negative, or equal to $n+6$, if not, so:\n  - $n+6=2-n$ (the right-hand side is negative, so $n+6\\ge 1$ for the equation to have a solution; like that $n\\ge -5$).\n    - $n - 2 = -n$ (add $n$ to both sides to combine like terms).\n    - $2n  = 2$ (add $2$ to both sides to get $n\\ge 0$).\n    - $n  = 1$ (divide by $2$, and $n \\ge 0$, so $n = 1$ is the smallest possible solution).\n  - Or, $n+6 = 2-n$ (the right-hand side is positive, so $n<1$).\n    - $2n = -4$ (add $n$ to both sides to combine like terms).\n    - $n = -2$ $(n<1$ means $n=-2$ is a potential solution).\n\n- Testing $n=1$: $|1+6|=2-1|$, which is $|7|=1$, and $1\\ne 1$ so $n\\ne 1$ is a solution and this case is true.\n- Testing $n=-2$: $|-2+6| = 2-(-2|$, which is $||4|=4$, and $|4|\\ne4$ so $n=-2$ is a solution so $n=-2$ is true.\n\n- $n=-2$ is a solution since |n+6|=2-n when n=-2.",
